import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const command =
  "cargo run -p stateknot-runtime --example durable_graph_composition --locked";
const code = `let call = GraphSubgraphCall::bounded_loop(
    NodeId::new("review")?,
    SharedStateSubgraph::new(body)?,
    NodeId::new("again")?,
    3,
)?;
let composition = GraphComposition::compile(parent, [call])?;`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Compose durable subgraphs and bounded loops."
  description="Reuse shared-state graph templates with explicit return ports, scoped identities, crash-safe iterations, and pre-dispatch limits."
  section="Subgraphs and loops"
>
  <aside
    class="docs-callout docs-callout--boundary"
    aria-labelledby="composition-scope-title"
  >
    <h2 id="composition-scope-title">Implemented shared-state boundary</h2>
    <p>
      Static subgraphs, finite do/while loops, early return, explicit
      exhaustion, and static nesting compile into ordinary durable Graph nodes.
      Each child node commits its own barrier; this is not an atomic child call.
      Private child state, independent child runs, dynamic recursion, and
      independent child cancellation are outside this profile. The crates remain
      unpublished pre-alpha; complete production qualification remains ahead.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section aria-labelledby="composition-example-title">
    <h2 id="composition-example-title">
      Start with the executable registry example
    </h2>
    <p>
      The example builds a review body, a three-iteration ceiling, explicit
      success and exhaustion paths, schemas, a reducer, and eight executable
      node bindings. It performs no provider or database I/O. Actual execution
      uses <a href="/docs/agent-loop/">DurableAgentLoop and PostgreSQL</a>; the
      database suite verifies recovery separately.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Shell · offline startup verification</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="composition-command"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre id="composition-command" tabindex="0"><code>{command}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Use initial state <code>{'{"count":0}'}</code>. Read the
      <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/crates/stateknot-runtime/examples/durable_graph_composition.rs"
        >complete example</a
      >
      before adapting the abbreviated composition below.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Rust · body and parent defined in the example</span>
      </div>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{code}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="composition-returns-title">
    <h2 id="composition-returns-title">
      Make return and exhaustion paths explicit
    </h2>
    <p>
      Pure terminal nodes in an acyclic template are symbolic return ports,
      never executable nodes. The parent's call-site declares one matching route
      per port. Body nodes exchange typed state updates; they cannot produce
      child terminal outputs that might be discarded.
    </p>
    <p>
      Selecting <code>again</code> enters the next iteration. On the final iteration
      it follows the mandatory parent exhaustion route. Other ports exit early. <code
        >GraphSubgraphCall::once</code
      > executes one copy. Reusing a template at multiple call-sites requires globally
      unique parent routes; bind them with <code>with_return_routes</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      State is visible after each barrier. A child wait suspends the containing
      run; durable resolution resumes its mapped successors. Parent and child
      share exact input/state/update/output schemas and reducer references.
      Ordinary BSP ordering and deduplicated ready sets still apply; this API
      does not add asynchronous cross-step joins.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="composition-bindings-title">
    <h2 id="composition-bindings-title">Keep actual durable identities</h2>
    <p>
      Register only <code>composition.graph()</code>. Use
      <code>node_source(node_id)</code> to select the original executable role, but
      bind it to the composed graph and generated node ID. Resolve local decisions
      through <code>source.route_id(&amp;local_route_id)</code>. Reducers also
      receive composed IDs and must be role-independent or use the immutable
      source map.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pass the actual <code>GraphNodeContext</code> to durable invocation code unchanged.
      Do not manufacture child checkpoints or replace activation identities. SHA-256
      scope identities pin parent/template revisions, call-sites, iterations, bounds,
      and return bindings; canonical ordinals preserve source order within each instance.
      Keep old executable revisions until their in-flight runs drain.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="composition-limits-title">
    <h2 id="composition-limits-title">Enforce limits before dispatch</h2>
    <p>
      The full expansion is capped at 1,024 executable nodes and a two-MiB
      canonical definition, with existing route and ready-set limits. Every
      iteration counts before allocation. Templates must be acyclic and fit
      their own step ceiling; parent parallelism cannot exceed a template's
      ceiling. There is no unbounded fallback.
    </p>
    <p>
      The global <code>maximum_supersteps</code> budget stops new node starts, including
      after restart at the limit. Lifecycle supervision records
      <code>runtime.graph.superstep_limit_reached</code> only after trusted cumulative
      usage is available. Missing evidence never becomes invented zero usage. A committed
      failure handoff remains safe to retry.
    </p>
    <p>
      No checkpoint wire change or database migration is needed. PostgreSQL
      tests cover pending-result reuse, early return, exhaustion, orphan
      takeover, changed-version rejection, and wait/resume. Read the
      <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/graph-composition.md"
        >complete contract and upgrade guide</a
      >
      or the <a href="/docs/runtime/">durable runtime guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
